{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-21072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-21072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-21072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "931142a4b61f92375d8f268a99285b7871ca4c62ce62cd72e5de2eaeaf15993d"} {"question" "q-21072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-21072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] ["text" "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\pi $"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-21072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-75278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-11,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+5,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-75278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=12\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-75278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "931142a4b61f92375d8f268a99285b7871ca4c62ce62cd72e5de2eaeaf15993d"} {"question" "q-75278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-75278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$"] ["text" "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-75278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{5},\\,y=9t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20{t}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=9\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "931142a4b61f92375d8f268a99285b7871ca4c62ce62cd72e5de2eaeaf15993d"} {"question" "q-122726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-1"] ["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=20"] ["math" "t=4"] ["math" "t=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "931142a4b61f92375d8f268a99285b7871ca4c62ce62cd72e5de2eaeaf15993d"} {"question" "q-122931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-122931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "931142a4b61f92375d8f268a99285b7871ca4c62ce62cd72e5de2eaeaf15993d"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064114/state.json" "file_sha256" "affe00d5ae8417b384327b08d427d3dd746915ddf99e9ae741581ece8a6d8485"} {"question" "q-350395" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [